[2026-08-23T20:47:04.825619+00:00] [stdout] {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":"setup-fft3d-fixed-geometry-opt-hot-20260823-58650d0e-2","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write a fast implementation of iterated batched 3D complex FFTs for eight\nfixed cube sizes. Your deliverable is code: a single self-contained C file,\ndriven through the exact Python wrapper protocol below. Specialize as hard\nas you like for the eight sizes -- constants known at compile time are yours\nto exploit; that is the entire point of the exercise.\n\n## The job\n\nFor each L in (6, 8, 13, 17, 23, 36, 45, 64): starting from a seeded batch\nx of shape (B_L, L, L, L) complex128, iterate m_L steps of\n\n    z = FFT3(x) + c_L\n    x <- z / (1 + |z|)            (elementwise, complex magnitude)\n\nwhere FFT3 is the forward, unnormalized 3D DFT of each L^3 volume,\n\n    out[b][k0][k1][k2] = sum_j in[b][j0][j1][j2]\n                         * exp(-2*pi*i*(k0*j0 + k1*j1 + k2*j2)/L),\n\nelement (b, x, y, z) at flat index ((b*L + x)*L + y)*L + z (C order), and\nc_L is a fixed seeded constant array (batch shape). The graded call is\n\n    def transform(seed: int, B6: int, B8: int, B13: int, B17: int, B23: int, B36: int, B45: int, B64: int, m6: int, m8: int, m13: int, m17: int, m23: int, m36: int, m45: int, m64: int) -> np.ndarray   # shape 2*(B6*216+B8*512+B13*2197+B17*4913+B23*12167+B36*46656+B45*91125+B64*262144), complex128\n\nwhich must return, concatenated in the order L = 6, 8, 13, 17, 23, 36, 45,\n64: for each L first the state after ONE step, then the state after m_L\nsteps (each block B_L*L^3 complex128, volumes flattened in C order).\nInputs and constants are generated inside the call as:\n\n    rng_x = np.random.default_rng(seed + L)\n    x0    = (rng_x.standard_normal((B_L, L, L, L))\n             + 1j * rng_x.standard_normal((B_L, L, L, L)))\n    rng_c = np.random.default_rng(1000 + L)\n    c_L   = 0.1 * (rng_c.standard_normal((B_L, L, L, L))\n             + 1j * rng_c.standard_normal((B_L, L, L, L)))\n\nUse the wrapper skeleton below verbatim except where marked; put ALL of\nyour optimization in the C file it compiles. Everything in solution.py\noutside the timed call (compilation, planning, table generation) is\nexcluded from your measured time by construction.\n\n## Prior work provided\n\nTwo rounds of prior work on this exact problem are provided; read them as\nbackground first.\n\n`/work/prior_work` contains the complete reconstructed solutions of SIXTEEN\nfirst-round attempts (`solutions/v5_*` and `solutions/v6_*`, each with its\n`solution.py`, `implementation.c` and/or the generator that emits it, and a\n`README.md` stating its graded score and approach), plus\n`IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md` summarizing the machine characteristics those\nattempts measured (FMA ports, issue caps, cache behavior, clocks, map\nstrategies).\n\n`/work/prior_work2` contains the five second-round (warm-start) solutions\n(`warm_*`, same layout), their audit addenda, and `ROUND2_NOTES.md` -- the\ndistilled findings of the second round: which prior engines were reused and\nrecombined, the final optimizations extracted from the fastest attempts, the\nhonest performance ledger (measured wall-time ratios, including which graded\nnumbers were later shown to be timing artifacts), and exactly what stands\nbetween the measured issue-cap equilibrium and the ISA-ideal target of this\nround.\n\nScores quoted in the READMEs are on earlier scoring curves; the measured\ntime ratios versus the reference are directly comparable. VERIFY anything\nyou rely on: the notes' measurements come from prior sessions on this\nmachine class, and the code was reconstructed from transcripts\n(generator-emitted C may differ in the last ulp of baked constants if\nregenerated off-host; the generators are included, and the correctness gates\nare yours to meet regardless of provenance). You may adopt, adapt, combine,\nor discard any of it -- make your own strategy choices, informed by but not\nbound to the previous work. All rules below apply unchanged to whatever you\nship; the provided code complied with Rule 1 and so must yours.\n\n## Reference definition\n\nThe correctness anchor is this straight-Python, extended-precision\nimplementation (numpy longdouble, ~1e-15 per step). It is the exact\ndefinition of the required output; the provided base.py is numerically\nequivalent to it but uses numpy's FFT for speed.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n_SIZES = (6, 8, 13, 17, 23, 36, 45, 64)\n_CLD, _LD = np.clongdouble, np.longdouble\n_PI = np.longdouble('3.14159265358979323846264338327950288')\n\ndef _dftmat(L):                         # forward unnormalized DFT matrix, exact mod-L twiddles\n    j = np.arange(L)\n    kj = np.outer(j, j) % L\n    ang = (-2 * _PI) * kj.astype(_LD) / _LD(L)\n    return np.cos(ang).astype(_CLD) + 1j * np.sin(ang).astype(_CLD)\n\ndef _fft3(x, W):                        # 3D DFT of each L^3 volume, applied axis by axis\n    y = np.einsum('bxyz,kx->bkyz', x, W)\n    y = np.einsum('bkyz,ly->bklz', y, W)\n    return np.einsum('bklz,mz->bklm', y, W)\n\ndef transform(seed, B6, B8, B13, B17, B23, B36, B45, B64,\n              m6, m8, m13, m17, m23, m36, m45, m64):\n    Bs = {6: B6, 8: B8, 13: B13, 17: B17, 23: B23, 36: B36, 45: B45, 64: B64}\n    ms = {6: m6, 8: m8, 13: m13, 17: m17, 23: m23, 36: m36, 45: m45, 64: m64}\n    outs = []\n    for L in _SIZES:\n        B, m = Bs[L], ms[L]\n        rng_x = np.random.default_rng(seed + L)\n        x = (rng_x.standard_normal((B, L, L, L))\n             + 1j * rng_x.standard_normal((B, L, L, L))).astype(_CLD)\n        rng_c = np.random.default_rng(1000 + L)\n        c = (0.1 * (rng_c.standard_normal((B, L, L, L))\n             + 1j * rng_c.standard_normal((B, L, L, L)))).astype(_CLD)\n        one = None\n        for k in range(m):\n            z = _fft3(x, _dftmat(L)) + c\n            x = z / (1.0 + np.abs(z))\n            if k == 0:\n                one = x.copy()\n        outs += [one.ravel().astype(np.complex128), x.ravel().astype(np.complex128)]\n    return np.concatenate(outs)\n```\n\n## Rules\n\n1. No FFT library calls in the iteration. Not FFTW, not MKL, not mkl_fft,\n   not scipy/numpy FFTs, not a vendor DFT of any kind: the transform\n   arithmetic must be your own. libm sin/cos during setup is fine. numpy\n   is allowed only for input generation and final assembly, not for the\n   transform or the elementwise map inside the iteration.\n   Solutions that ship, copy, or load any FFT library binary in the graded\n   path score zero on audit.\n2. Single-threaded. No OpenMP, no pthreads. One core.\n3. All precomputation goes in your create/setup path at import time\n   (twiddle tables, permutation tables, generated code, self-tuning over\n   variants -- as expensive as you like).\n4. Repeatable: same inputs, same answer every time.\n5. Accuracy: the one-step blocks must match the reference to relative L2\n   error < 1e-14 per block -- this is the correctness bar; a careful fp64\n   implementation lands near 1e-15. The m_L-step blocks are gated PER SIZE:\n\n       L:        6      8      13     17     23     36     45     64\n       tol:    1e-4   3e-6   1e-9   1e-10  1e-10  1e-10  1e-10  1e-10\n\n   The iteration is chaotic, so rounding differences between correct fp64\n   implementations grow with the chain length; each gate above sits two and a half\n   to three orders of magnitude ABOVE the honest fp64 divergence measured\n   between independent correct implementations at that size's chain length -- and more than three orders BELOW the amplified\n   error of any reduced-precision chain. Carry the entire iteration in\n   IEEE double precision: computing interior steps in single (or otherwise\n   reduced) precision fails these gates at every size, as does skipping or\n   approximating steps. Reassociating additions is expected; do not trade\n   away digits in the one-step blocks.\n   The correctness anchor is the extended-precision reference defined in\n   \"Reference definition\" below. The provided base.py computes exactly that\n   transform but calls numpy's FFT (pocketfft) instead of the longdouble\n   matrix-DFT, purely for speed as a grading reference; the two are\n   numerically equivalent, agreeing to ~1e-15 per step, and base.py is the\n   implementation the grader actually times and checks against. base.py is\n   the reference, not the target: your graded solution may not use any FFT\n   library (Rule 1), so beating it means writing your own transform.\n6. Your solution must be fully self-contained at grading time: assume gcc\n   and numpy are available but NO network and NO pre-installed packages\n   beyond the standard scientific stack. You may use pip in your working\n   session for experimentation, but the graded import must not need it.\n   Keep /workdir small: grading snapshots it and rejects working\n   directories over 500 MiB, scoring zero. Install experimentation\n   packages elsewhere (pip --user or a /tmp prefix) and remove large\n   build artifacts before you finish.\n\n## Wrapper skeleton (use verbatim except the C source and the marked call)\n\n```python\nimport os, subprocess, ctypes\nimport numpy as np\ntry:\n    os.sched_setaffinity(0, {0})   # pin to one core: timing fairness\nexcept Exception:\n    pass\n\n_here = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))\n_c    = os.path.join(_here, 'implementation.c')   # <- your C file, written by you\n_so   = os.path.join(_here, 'implementation.so')\nif not os.path.exists(_so):\n    subprocess.run(['gcc', '-O3', '-march=native', '-shared', '-fPIC',\n                    _c, '-o', _so, '-lm'], check=True)   # add flags if needed\n_lib = ctypes.CDLL(_so)\n# ... your ctypes bindings and per-L create/plan calls here (import time) ...\n\n_SIZES = (6, 8, 13, 17, 23, 36, 45, 64)\n\ndef transform(seed, B6, B8, B13, B17, B23, B36, B45, B64, m6, m8, m13, m17, m23, m36, m45, m64):\n    Bs = {6: B6, 8: B8, 13: B13, 17: B17, 23: B23, 36: B36, 45: B45, 64: B64}\n    ms = {6: m6, 8: m8, 13: m13, 17: m17, 23: m23, 36: m36, 45: m45, 64: m64}\n    outs = []\n    for L in _SIZES:\n        B = Bs[L]\n        rng_x = np.random.default_rng(seed + L)\n        x0 = (rng_x.standard_normal((B, L, L, L))\n              + 1j * rng_x.standard_normal((B, L, L, L)))\n        rng_c = np.random.default_rng(1000 + L)\n        c = 0.1 * (rng_c.standard_normal((B, L, L, L))\n              + 1j * rng_c.standard_normal((B, L, L, L)))\n        one, final = _run(L, B, ms[L], x0, c)   # <- your call into C\n        outs += [one.ravel(), final.ravel()]\n    return np.concatenate(outs)\n```\n\nThe grader times fresh calls of transform() (import excluded), takes the\nbest of several identically-measured runs of your code and of a held-out\nreference implementation on the same parameters, and scores the time\nratio r = yours/reference on this exact curve (authoritative -- it\nsupersedes any generic scoring summary appended below): slower than the\nreference scores 0; matching it scores 0.1; the score rises linearly to\n1.0 at r <= 0.078 -- that is, FULL MARKS REQUIRE 12.8x THE REFERENCE. This\nanchor is the ISA-IDEAL roofline: best-known FFT operation counts executed\nat this machine's ISA-ideal 32 flops/cycle (both 512-bit FMA pipes issuing\nevery cycle), T_perfect = 1.05 s. It sits deliberately above the previous\nround's anchor (the measured ~2.1-vector-uop/cycle issue-cap roofline,\n7.3x): the best genuine prior attempts reached ~4-4.9x the reference, and closing the rest\nmeans structurally beating the measured issue equilibrium -- register-resident\ndata flow that keeps both FMA pipes fed without load/store or shuffle uops\nstealing issue slots.\n\nOne more degree of freedom: the 12.8x anchor is computed from the best-KNOWN\nDFT operation counts (split-radix, PFA/Winograd modules, tangent FFT). The\ntrue arithmetic complexity of the DFT is an OPEN PROBLEM -- the bound is\nalgorithmic, not information-theoretic -- so you are free to design NEW\ntransform algorithms with lower operation counts, not just better\nimplementations of known ones. Anything is admissible provided you prove it\ncorrect: derive the algorithm explicitly in your working notes, and it must\ncompute the exact DFT defined above within the accuracy gates of Rule 5\n(the 1e-14 one-step gate is the operational correctness bar; Rule 1's\nno-FFT-library constraint applies unchanged). A genuinely lower-count\ncorrect algorithm executed at the same issue rate would beat the anchor\nitself. The objective is to be as fast as possible.\n\nDELIVERABLE\n\nWrite your code to /workdir/solution.py. It must define exactly this interface:\n\n    def transform(seed: int, B6: int, B8: int, B13: int, B17: int, B23: int, B36: int, B45: int, B64: int, m6: int, m8: int, m13: int, m17: int, m23: int, m36: int, m45: int, m64: int) -> np.ndarray[shape=(2*(B6*216+B8*512+B13*2197+B17*4913+B23*12167+B36*46656+B45*91125+B64*262144),), dtype=complex128]\n\nsolution.py may import other modules you create in /workdir and packages you install.\n\nA simple, trusted implementation of the same interface is provided at\n/workdir/base.py as a starting point. It defines correct behavior; your\ngoal is to beat its cost.\n\nGRADING\n\nYour code will be executed on parameter values you cannot see, alongside two\nreference implementations, on this machine. Grading steps:\n\n1. Correctness: output format (type and shape per the interface), problem\n   constraints, and content checks against a trusted reference implementation.\n   Any failure scores 0.\n2. Cost: the wall-clock time your code takes to produce the output (process startup is subtracted via a baseline run; best of several repetitions).\n3. Score: increases smoothly with your improvement over the trusted reference;\n   matching a strong held-out implementation earns half credit, and the score\n   keeps growing (with diminishing returns) beyond it. Aim as high as you can.\n\nRULES\n\n- Everything runs in this container; you may use any software you can install\n  here, threads and subprocesses included.\n- Your code must compute its output from its inputs. Hardcoding outputs,\n  memoizing across runs, or interfering with the measurement scores 0.\n- Do not modify files you did not create in /workdir; grading uses pristine\n  copies of everything it needs.\n"}],"isError":false}}
